{"name":"Stained Glass","hex":"#556682","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"stained glass","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Glass that has been coloured, either by painting or by fusing pigments into its structure.","The use of such glass to construct decorative windows, especially in churches."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stained_glass"}],"entries":[{"title":"Stained glass","blocks":[{"text":"Stained glass refers to coloured glass as a material or art and architectural works created from it. Although it is traditionally made in flat panels and used as windows, the creations of modern stained glass artists also include three-dimensional structures and sculpture. Modern vernacular usage has often extended the term \"stained glass\" to include domestic lead light and objets d'art created from glasswork, for example in the famous lamps of Louis Comfort Tiffany."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Chartres_RosetteNord_121_DSC08241.jpg/330px-Chartres_RosetteNord_121_DSC08241.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stained_glass"},{"title":"Stained Glass (TV series)","blocks":[{"text":"Stained Glass is a 2004 South Korean television drama series starring Lee Dong-gun, Kim Ha-neul, and Kim Sung-soo. It aired on SBS from December 1, 2004 to February 3, 2005 on Wednesdays and Thursdays at 21:55 for 18 episodes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/2/2e/Stained_Glass-poster.jpg/330px-Stained_Glass-poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stained_Glass_(TV_series)"},{"title":"Stain","blocks":[{"text":"A stain is a discoloration that can be clearly distinguished from the surface, material, or medium it is found upon. They are caused by the chemical or physical interaction of two dissimilar materials. Accidental staining may make materials appear used, degraded or permanently unclean. Intentional staining is used in biochemical research, and for artistic effect, such as in wood staining, rust staining and stained glass."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Patternspage.jpg/330px-Patternspage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stain"},{"title":"Glass","blocks":[{"text":"Glass is an amorphous (non-crystalline) solid. Because it is often transparent and chemically inert, glass has found widespread practical, technological, and decorative use in window panes, tableware, and optics. Some common objects made of glass are named after the material, e.g., a \"glass\" for drinking, \"glasses\" for vision correction, and a \"magnifying glass\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Fassade_Wilhelmstrasse_65%2C_Berlin-Mitte%2C_160417%2C_ako.jpg/330px-Fassade_Wilhelmstrasse_65%2C_Berlin-Mitte%2C_160417%2C_ako.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glass"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Magi_Herod_MNMA_Cl23532.jpg/500px-Magi_Herod_MNMA_Cl23532.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Magi_Herod_MNMA_Cl23532.jpg/1280px-Magi_Herod_MNMA_Cl23532.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Magi_Herod_MNMA_Cl23532.jpg/500px-Magi_Herod_MNMA_Cl23532.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Grisaille stained glass (15th century)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMagi_Herod_MNMA_Cl23532.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Le_Mesnil-Villeman_%E2%80%93_%C3%89glise_Saint-Pierre_%E2%80%